1 NOTE {geni:about_me} After officer training at the Swedish Naval Academy, he travelled to England and applied to the Royal Navy where he was employed between 1776 and 1782.  
2 CONT
2 CONT From about 1782 he worked as a merchant in the City of London. In 1800, he was hired by the Admiralty as a  translator: a position he held until 1829.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first marriage (1782-1796) was to Ann Richardson (d.1796). In 1798 he married Jane Vazeille Smith (1770 -1849). Nine children were born to each marriage, although by the time of Christian's death in 1835 there were only three 
2 CONC surviving children from the second marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He converted to Methodism in 1780 becoming a Methodist preacher and eventually a director and trustee of  the British and Foreign Bible Society; the London Missionary Society and the Swedish Protestant Church in London. He is buri
2 CONC ed in the City Road Chapel, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://bit.ly/UrZHdU
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Capt. Kristian (Christopher Christian) Sundius Born 25 Aug 1754 in Allerum, Malmöhus, Sverigemap ANCESTORS ancestors Son of Johannes Nicolaus Sundius and Cecilia Blanxius Brother of Johannes Sundius, Ivar Sundius, Brita Christina 
2 CONC Sundius, Mathias Sundius, Helena Catharina Sundius, Eva Maria Sundius and Maria Christina Sundius Husband of Ann (Richardson) Sundius — married 27 Jul 1782 in St George in the East, Stepney, London, Englandmap Husband of Jane Vaze
2 CONC ille (Smith) Sundius — married 4 Jul 1798 in Saint Andrew, Newcastle Upon Tyne, Northumberland, Englandmap DESCENDANTS descendants Father of Carolina Ann Richardson Sundius, Ann Richardson Sundius, Barbara Cicelia (Sundius) Smith,
2 CONC  John Frederick Sundius, Henrietta Sundius, Frederika Maria Sundius, William Pulteney Sundius, Louisa Ann Sundius, Christopher Christian Smith Sundius, Charles Christian Sundius, Jane Wilhelmina Sundius, Henry Augustus Sundius, La
2 CONC ura Gustava Mee Sundius and Elizabeth Mary (Sundius) Minchin Died 2 Mar 1835 in Stoke Newington, London, England, United Kingdommap Profile manager: Phil Richardson  Profile last modified 2 Jun 2021 | Created 21 Jul 2014 | Last si
2 CONC gnificant change: 2 Jun 2021 03:33: Linda (Groom) Prole edited the Biography for Kristian Sundius (1754-1835). (Fixing typo) [Thank Linda for this]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Born in 1754, he was the son of Johannes Nicolaus (Swedish form: Johan) Sundius, who was vicar in Allerum parish, and Cecilia Blanxius (Blanxia). Kristian had eight older siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristian was baptised on 27 August 1754 at Allerum, Malmöhus, Sweden[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Ann Richardson (the daughter of James Richardson) on 27 July 1782 at St George in the East, Tower Hamlets, London, England, by Licence. The Rector was Herbert Mayo, and the witnesses were Barbara Bruce and Aron Matthews
2 CONC . Ann and Christian both signed their names.
2 CONT
2 CONT They would go on to have nine children, some of whom died young. His wife Ann died in 1796.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is taken from the British Online Archives and has been computer generated:
2 CONT
2 CONT ""On July 27th 1782 Mr Sundius married a young lady of the name of Miss Ann Richardson. At the time of first acquaintance with her was about going to India with the lady of general Matthews with daughters had educated was intended
2 CONC  she join aunts who well her uncle resided in India. Miss Richardson loved God and resolved to cast in lot for life with Mr Sundius have the following tribute to excellence in own words was a child of God and had profited greatly 
2 CONC by hearing Mr Wesley and his blessed assistants preach at the Old Foundery and notwithstanding ill-health continued a single-hearted believer in Jesus to the end of days lady appears to made one voyage with beloved husband was to 
2 CONC the West Indies. At the island of Grenada were treated with marked distinction and had the honour of being invited to reside at the Government-house. Mrs Sundius the mother of nine children and died in peace in May 96 a painful il
2 CONC lness. On July 4th 1798 Mr Sundius entered the marriage-state with Miss Jane Vizeille Smith daughter of William Smith Esq of Newcastle-upon-Tyne union also blessed with nine children.""
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Jane Vazeille Smith (the daughter of William Smith) were married on 4 July 1798 at Saint Andrew, Newcastle Upon Tyne, Northumberland, England[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1800's he and his family lived at 7 Fen Court, Fenchurch Street. His occupation was recorded as Translator And Notarypubiic.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1815 he was a merchant of Devonshire-square when he was adjudged Bankrupt. The family later lived in Palatine House, Stoke Newington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher was in Partnership with Frederick Pfeiffer at 16 Finch Lane, Cornhill, as notaries and translators of languages. Their partnership was dissolved in December 1828.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Sundius died in March 1835, having outlived all but five of his children and his second wife Jane.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Evening Chronicle 07 March 1835, Page 4: DIED On the 2d Inst., at Stoke Newington, in his 81st year, Christopher Sundius, Esq.[3] He was buried on 10 March 1835 in the family grave in City Road Chapel (Wesleyan), City Road, ne
2 CONC xt to John Wesley's grave[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography from Findagrave "A Wesleyan minister, Christopher (Christian) Sundius (1754-1835) was born in southern Sweden and went to university and Naval College. He travelled to England and offered his services to the British Navy
2 CONC . He continued his service there until after his first marriage in 1782. Christopher attended services at John Wesley’s Foundery in Tabernacle Street in 1777 and was converted in 1780. As he had an extensive knowledge of foreign l
2 CONC anguages he was appointed to the Admiralty in 1800. He held this post until 1829. Sundius was one of the founding members of the British and Foreign Bible Society, which began in 1804 and was also one the first promotors and direc
2 CONC tors of the London Missionary Society. As trustee of the Swedish Protestant Church in London he assisted in the formation of the Society of Friends of Foreigners in Distress. After the death of his first wife in 1796, Christopher 
2 CONC married Jane Vazeille Smith, the step-grandaughter of John Wesley. He had nine children with each wife. Sundius died in March 1831 and is buried in the family grave in City Road Chapel, next to John Wesley's grave."
2 CONT
