1 OCCU Metodistpastor, Methodist Minister
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fil. dr.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM HENSCHEN, editor of Sändebudet, was born April 11, 1842 in Upsala, Sweden. His parents were district administrator Lars Wilhelm Henschen and his wife Augusta Munck af Rosenschöld.
2 CONT
2 CONT After several years of private instruction, he was entered at the cathedral school at Upsala in 1850.  He graduated from the University of Upsala on Dec. 9, 1857, finished his doctorate studies in 1862, and, after a defense of his
2 CONC  thesis in May, 1863, he was awarded the degree of Doctor of Philosophy.  Dr. Henschen subsequently pursued scientific and medical studies for three years at the universities of Upsala and Berlin.  In 1866 he was appointed special
2 CONC  teacher at the Lund collegiate institute and in 1867 he became an instructor in the Helsingborg collegiate institute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emigrating in September, 1870, to the United States. he settled in Florida.  After two years he went to New York, where he became in October, 1873, editor of Nordstjernan.  From June, 1874, until August, 1875. Dr. Henschen was edi
2 CONC tor and part owner of Norden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Henschen joined the Dean Street Methodist Church in the spring of 1875.  The following September he moved to Chicago.  He now became a teacher in the Swedish M. E. Theological Seminary and also editor of Sändebudet, the church
2 CONC  organ, serving in the latter position until September, 1882, when he resigned.  A year later he withdrew from the seminary and became pastor of the Jamestown, N.Y., church. He passed the winter of 1884 in southern Florida.  The n
2 CONC ext spring Dr. Henschen left for Sweden where he labored as editor and teacher in Stockholm and Upsala until 1889.  Returning to this country, he has ever since been editor of Sändebudet, with the exception of four years, when he 
2 CONC was pastor of the Swedish M. E. Church at Galva, Ill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Henschen was in 1868 united in marriage with Hilda Johanna Maria Liljebjörn.  They have had eleven children, of whom the seven surviving ones are: Henry Samuel Henschen, cashier of the State Bank of Chicago, Gustave E. Hensche
2 CONC n, a physician in Georgetown, Tex., Mrs. Signe Elizabeth Reese, Mrs. Ellen Augusta Herbert, Mrs. Hanna Linnea Grant, Agnes Lillian Henschen and Hilda Irene Henschen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref.:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1920 Census indicates living in Chicago, IL
2 CONT
2 CONT Age 77, immigration in 1870, and naturalization in 1876
2 CONT
2 CONT 1910 Census indicates living in Chicago, IL
2 CONT
2 CONT Only Hilda at home (22)
