2 NOTE As a married noblewoman of her era, Janet would not bear a standalone personal coat of arms. Instead, her heraldic identity depends entirely on which phase of her life the image represents, using impaled arms (where the shield is 
3 CONC split vertically down the middle):
3 CONT
3 CONT 1. Heraldic Mechanics of Her Arms
3 CONT
3 CONT An accurate historical shield for her must feature:
3 CONT
3 CONT '''Dexter Side (The viewer's left):''' The arms of her husband—either the MacLean of Duart quarterly arms (featuring the rock, the hand holding the cross, the galley, and the salmon) or the MacLeod of Dunvegan arms (the castle/tow
3 CONC er).
3 CONT
3 CONT '''Sinister Side (The viewer's right):''' Her paternal House of Argyll arms—the iconic Campbell gyronny of eight (gold and black triangles), quartered with the black galley of Lorne.
3 CONT
3 CONT 2. Red Flags to Look Out For in the Geni Album
3 CONT
3 CONT If the image uploaded to that link shows any of the following, it is a modern database error and should be removed from her Master Profile:
3 CONT
3 CONT '''A Standalone Campbell Shield:''' If it is just a standard, un-impaled Campbell shield, it belongs to her father or brothers, not to her as a married woman.
3 CONT
3 CONT '''A Crest or Helmet:''' In 16th-century Scottish heraldry, women do not bear crests, helmets, or mottos, as they did not participate in physical combat or tournaments. An authentic ladies' armorial bearing will display the shield
3 CONC  alone (often in a lozenge or oval shape if single/widowed, or a standard impaled shield if married).
3 CONT
3 CONT '''Modern "Clan Crest" Badges:''' If the image is a modern strap-and-buckle badge containing a boar's head (Campbell) or a tower (MacLeod), it is a modern commercial product, not an archival coat of arms.
3 CONT
3 CONT If the image at that link shows an impaled shield combining MacLean or MacLeod on the left with the Campbell/Lorne quarters on the right, it is a valid representation of her married arms.
